   "published": "Sat, 28 Nov 2026 14:33:22 +0530",
   "_dt": 1795856602.0,
   "ageDays": 0.05,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The MoU was signed in the presence of MSDE Secretary Debashree Mukherjee by MSDE Deputy Secretary Indu Bhusan Lenka and AP State Skill Development Corporation (APSSDC) Executive Director D.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅后解锁这些内容。谅解备忘录在Maharashtra技能发展部秘书Debashree Mukherjee见证下签署，Maharashtra技能发展部副秘书长Indu Bhusan Lenka和Andhra Pradesh邦技能发展公司（APSSDC）执行董事D.出席。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The MoU was signed in the presence of MSDE Secretary Debashree Mukherjee by MSDE Deputy Secretary Indu Bhusan Lenka and AP State Skill Development Corporation (APSSDC) Executive Director D. Manohar, representing their respective organisations. In a statement on Friday (November 28, 2026), Mr. Manohar said the proposed centre will provide internationally aligned skills training, foreign-language instruction, cultural orientation, skill assessment and certification, visa and documentation assistance, pre-departure training and overseas placement support. It will focus on sectors such as healthcare, engineering, construction, logistics, hospitality and emerging green jobs by leveraging the state’s existing training institutions, he added. The ED informed that since June 2024, the programme had trained 525 candidates, assessed 457 and certified 189, besides facilitating 1,993 overseas placements. Another 3,263 candidates were currently undergoing training, he said. Through initiatives such as Project Vasudha, Project Sarvasethu and Project Blue Diamond, the state was creating overseas employment pathways in Germany, Japan, Russia, Italy and GCC countries, he said. The establishment of SIIC is expected to strengthen Centre-State cooperation and position Andhra Pradesh as a major hub for globally skilled and certified manpower, ensuring safe, structured and legal overseas employment opportunities for its youth, he added. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
